From 9b3f621a6b8baa836099353359cb8dd47f6ad32e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Simon <ondrej@dorhawk.software>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 09:45:51 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 52/54] winemac.drv: Judge a Mac window tool's change to a
 maximized window.

Window tools such as Rectangle change a window's frame through the
Accessibility API, in steps: the size, the position, then the size
again. For a maximized window the driver applied each frame and left
the window maximized, so a window tiled to half the screen still showed
the restore button and restored to its old rect, and a window moved to
another display stayed maximized but restored to the display it left.
macOS may also hold the size the tool asks for to the display the window
is leaving, so the window didn't fill the new display.

The Cocoa window now tells the back end when its frame has stopped
changing (WINDOW_FRAME_SETTLED, 0.3 s after the last change), and the
driver judges the frame as a whole, against where the change began, if
the window filled a display then:

- Not filling a display, the same one or another (a half, a third, a
  frame with gaps around it): the window isn't maximized any more, as on
  Windows when the user resizes a maximized window. It restores to its
  new frame.
- Moved to another display whole: filling it, the size it was (a move
  without a new size), or as wide as it and as tall as it was (what
  macOS leaves of a maximize there, holding the height to the display
  the window left): it stays maximized, fills that display, and the rect
  it restores to moves along to that display, as with Windows'
  Win+Shift+arrow.

A window fills a display when its edges reach the work area's, give or
take 2 points, and its top is no more than a menu bar lower than it was
at the start: a Mac title bar sits above the visible rect, and a display
that isn't the main one has a menu bar its work area doesn't leave out.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
